@@ -0,0 +1,63 @@
+From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
+Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 13:28:05 +0200
+Subject: rockchip: fix IO domain supplies of NanoPi R2S Plus
+
+The NanoPi R2S Plus device tree inherits the IO domain supplies of the
+NanoPi R2S. On the R2S Plus two of them are wired differently. VCCIO4
+feeds the RGMII pins of the RTL8211F and is supplied by vcc_io_33.
+VCCIO6 feeds the M.2 Wi-Fi slot and is supplied by vcc_18. The GRF
+therefore configured the GMAC pins for 1.8 V while they are powered
+from 3.3 V.
+
+Describe the actual supplies.
+
+Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
+Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
+
+diff --git a/target/linux/rockchip/patches-6.18/166-arm64-dts-rockchip-fix-IO-domain-supplies-of-NanoPi-R2S-Plus.patch b/target/linux/rockchip/patches-6.18/166-arm64-dts-rockchip-fix-IO-domain-supplies-of-NanoPi-R2S-Plus.patch
+new file mode 100644
+index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..37905892ca783fce8e97f5d8faf2a8a2188c0e3d
+--- /dev/null
++++ b/target/linux/rockchip/patches-6.18/166-arm64-dts-rockchip-fix-IO-domain-supplies-of-NanoPi-R2S-Plus.patch
+@@ -0,0 +1,41 @@
++From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
++From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
++Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 09:47:59 +0200
++Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: rockchip: fix IO domain supplies of NanoPi R2S
++ Plus
++
++The NanoPi R2S Plus inherits the IO domain supplies of the NanoPi R2S,
++but its board wires two of them differently. On the R2S Plus schematic
++(revision 2309, sheet 6) the VCCIO4 ball is tied to VCC_IO_33 and the
++VCCIO6 ball to VCC_18. On the R2S the same balls are tied to the PHY
++I/O rail and to VCC_IO_33.
++
++VCCIO4 powers the GPIO1 B, C and D pins that carry the RGMII signals.
++The R2S Plus uses an RTL8211F-CG whose RGMII I/O supply is fed from
++the 3.3 V rail (sheet 11), while the R2S uses an RTL8211E. VCCIO6
++powers the GPIO3 A and B pins that carry the SDIO bus and the control
++lines of the M.2 Wi-Fi slot at 1.8 V. The vendor device tree describes
++the same two supplies.
++
++With the inherited supplies the io-domain driver selects 1.8 V for
++the GMAC pins while they are powered from 3.3 V. On the board the GRF
++showed the 1.8 V setting before this change and the 3.3 V setting
++after it. Describe the actual supplies.
++
++Fixes: b8c028782922 ("arm64: dts: rockchip: Add DTS for FriendlyARM NanoPi R2S Plus")
++Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
++---
++ arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3328-nanopi-r2s-plus.dts | 5 +++++
++ 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)
++
++--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3328-nanopi-r2s-plus.dts
+++++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3328-nanopi-r2s-plus.dts
++@@ -46,3 +46,8 @@
++ 		};
++ 	};
++ };
+++
+++&io_domains {
+++	vccio4-supply = <&vcc_io_33>;
+++	vccio6-supply = <&vcc_18>;
+++};
diff --git a/patches/openwrt/0022-rockchip-raise-sdmmc0ext-drive-strength-on-RK3328.patch b/patches/openwrt/0022-rockchip-raise-sdmmc0ext-drive-strength-on-RK3328.patch
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..653bf059
--- /dev/null
+++ b/patches/openwrt/0022-rockchip-raise-sdmmc0ext-drive-strength-on-RK3328.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,84 @@
+From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
+Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 13:45:58 +0200
+Subject: rockchip: raise sdmmc0ext drive strength on RK3328
+
+rk3328.dtsi configures the sdmmc0ext pins with 4 mA. The sdmmc0 pins
+use 8 mA, and so does the vendor kernel for the sdmmc0ext clock, command
+and 4-bit data pins. No in-tree board uses sdmmc_ext. On the NanoPi R2S
+Plus the RTL8822CS on sdmmc_ext fails SDR104 tuning at 4 mA. In high
+speed mode the link is barely usable with round trip times of 90 to
+210 ms.
+
+Use 8 mA like sdmmc0. Tuning passes and the round trip time drops to
+about 3 ms.
+
+Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
+Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5-1
+
+diff --git a/target/linux/rockchip/patches-6.18/167-arm64-dts-rockchip-raise-sdmmc0ext-drive-strength-on-RK3328.patch b/target/linux/rockchip/patches-6.18/167-arm64-dts-rockchip-raise-sdmmc0ext-drive-strength-on-RK3328.patch
+new file mode 100644
+index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..21596ed314aca2df4e7c7a9f7a79fd6baa375c15
+--- /dev/null
++++ b/target/linux/rockchip/patches-6.18/167-arm64-dts-rockchip-raise-sdmmc0ext-drive-strength-on-RK3328.patch
+@@ -0,0 +1,61 @@
++From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
++From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
++Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 19:08:24 +0200
++Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: rockchip: raise sdmmc0ext drive strength on
++ RK3328
++
++The RK3328 sdmmc0ext pinctrl entries use a drive strength of 4 mA. The
++sdmmc0 entries for the same pin roles use 8 mA. The vendor kernel uses
++8 mA for the sdmmc0ext clock, command and 4-bit data pins as well. No
++in-tree board uses sdmmc_ext so far.
++
++On the NanoPi R2S Plus an RTL8822CS SDIO module sits on sdmmc_ext. At
++4 mA the host fails SDR104 tuning on this card. In high speed mode the
++link is barely usable with round trip times of 90 to 210 ms.
++
++Raise the sdmmc0ext clock, command and data pins to 8 mA like sdmmc0.
++Leave the card detect and write protect pins at 4 mA like sdmmc0.
++With 8 mA SDR104 tuning passes and the link works fine.
++
++Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
++---
++ arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3328.dtsi | 14 +++++++-------
++ 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)
++
++--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3328.dtsi
+++++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3328.dtsi
++@@ -1690,11 +1690,11 @@
++ 
++ 		sdmmc0ext {
++ 			sdmmc0ext_clk: sdmmc0ext-clk {
++-				rockchip,pins = <3 RK_PA2 3 &pcfg_pull_none_4ma>;
+++				rockchip,pins = <3 RK_PA2 3 &pcfg_pull_none_8ma>;
++ 			};
++ 
++ 			sdmmc0ext_cmd: sdmmc0ext-cmd {
++-				rockchip,pins = <3 RK_PA0 3 &pcfg_pull_up_4ma>;
+++				rockchip,pins = <3 RK_PA0 3 &pcfg_pull_up_8ma>;
++ 			};
++ 
++ 			sdmmc0ext_wrprt: sdmmc0ext-wrprt {
++@@ -1706,15 +1706,15 @@
++ 			};
++ 
++ 			sdmmc0ext_bus1: sdmmc0ext-bus1 {
++-				rockchip,pins = <3 RK_PA4 3 &pcfg_pull_up_4ma>;
+++				rockchip,pins = <3 RK_PA4 3 &pcfg_pull_up_8ma>;
++ 			};
++ 
++ 			sdmmc0ext_bus4: sdmmc0ext-bus4 {
++ 				rockchip,pins =
++-					<3 RK_PA4 3 &pcfg_pull_up_4ma>,
++-					<3 RK_PA5 3 &pcfg_pull_up_4ma>,
++-					<3 RK_PA6 3 &pcfg_pull_up_4ma>,
++-					<3 RK_PA7 3 &pcfg_pull_up_4ma>;
+++					<3 RK_PA4 3 &pcfg_pull_up_8ma>,
+++					<3 RK_PA5 3 &pcfg_pull_up_8ma>,
+++					<3 RK_PA6 3 &pcfg_pull_up_8ma>,
+++					<3 RK_PA7 3 &pcfg_pull_up_8ma>;
++ 			};
++ 
++ 			sdmmc0ext_pins: sdmmc0ext-pins {
diff --git a/patches/openwrt/0023-rockchip-add-Wi-Fi-support-for-NanoPi-R2S-Plus.patch b/patches/openwrt/0023-rockchip-add-Wi-Fi-support-for-NanoPi-R2S-Plus.patch
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..8016f0e7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/patches/openwrt/0023-rockchip-add-Wi-Fi-support-for-NanoPi-R2S-Plus.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,134 @@
+From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
+Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 13:45:58 +0200
+Subject: rockchip: add Wi-Fi support for NanoPi R2S Plus
+
+FriendlyElec offers an RTL8822CS SDIO module as an option for the M.2
+E-key slot of the NanoPi R2S Plus. The slot is wired to sdmmc_ext and
+signals at 1.8 V from the VCCIO6 domain. Describe the slot, its power
+sequence and its supplies in the device tree, and ship the rtw88 SDIO
+driver and wpad with the image.
+
+The card runs in high speed mode because it sets S18A on the already
+1.8 V bus and then fails the CMD11 voltage switch. A separate mmc core
+change enables SDR104 and goes to linux-mmc first. Measured in high
+speed mode against an ath10k access point: about 3 ms round trip time,
+30 Mbit/s up and 65 Mbit/s down.
+
+Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
+Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
+
+diff --git a/target/linux/rockchip/image/armv8.mk b/target/linux/rockchip/image/armv8.mk
+index 3e973b8202da836217ced3af81d2ecc2f5ab7ffb..9d26b336af0abc74a855df7c0aa93420f0628674 100644
+--- a/target/linux/rockchip/image/armv8.mk
++++ b/target/linux/rockchip/image/armv8.mk
+@@ -124,7 +124,7 @@ define Device/friendlyarm_nanopi-r2s-plus
+   $(Device/rk3328)
+   DEVICE_VENDOR := FriendlyARM
+   DEVICE_MODEL := NanoPi R2S Plus
+-  DEVICE_PACKAGES := kmod-usb-net-rtl8152
++  DEVICE_PACKAGES := kmod-rtw88-8822cs kmod-usb-net-rtl8152 wpad-basic-mbedtls
+ endef
+ TARGET_DEVICES += friendlyarm_nanopi-r2s-plus
+ 
+diff --git a/target/linux/rockchip/patches-6.18/168-arm64-dts-rockchip-add-Wi-Fi-to-NanoPi-R2S-Plus.patch b/target/linux/rockchip/patches-6.18/168-arm64-dts-rockchip-add-Wi-Fi-to-NanoPi-R2S-Plus.patch
+new file mode 100644
+index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..3b4aeef3ac7e62ae34b26b2ae27619cb652fb762
+--- /dev/null
++++ b/target/linux/rockchip/patches-6.18/168-arm64-dts-rockchip-add-Wi-Fi-to-NanoPi-R2S-Plus.patch
+@@ -0,0 +1,96 @@
++From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
++From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
++Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 09:48:14 +0200
++Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: rockchip: add Wi-Fi to NanoPi R2S Plus
++
++The NanoPi R2S Plus has an M.2 E-key slot that FriendlyElec populates
++with an RTL8822CS SDIO Wi-Fi module as an option. The slot is wired to
++the sdmmc_ext controller. Its SDIO bus and control lines sit on GPIO3 A
++and B, which belong to the VCCIO6 domain. The board feeds that domain
++with 1.8 V, as described by the preceding IO domain fix, so the slot
++signals at 1.8 V. WL_REG_ON is on GPIO3_B0, WL_HOST_WAKE on GPIO3_A1
++and SUSCLK is fed by the 32.768 kHz clock output of the RK805. A
++dedicated buck converter powers the module with 3.3 V whenever
++vcc_io_33 is up.
++
++Describe the slot in the board device tree, as the vendor device tree
++does. A board without the module only logs that the non-removable
++card failed to initialise.
++
++Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
++---
++ .../dts/rockchip/rk3328-nanopi-r2s-plus.dts   | 59 +++++++++++++++++++
++ 1 file changed, 59 insertions(+)
++
++--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3328-nanopi-r2s-plus.dts
+++++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3328-nanopi-r2s-plus.dts
++@@ -17,6 +17,25 @@
++ 	aliases {
++ 		mmc1 = &emmc;
++ 	};
+++
+++	vdd_wifi_3v3: regulator-vdd-wifi-3v3 {
+++		compatible = "regulator-fixed";
+++		regulator-name = "vdd_wifi_3v3";
+++		regulator-always-on;
+++		regulator-boot-on;
+++		regulator-min-microvolt = <3300000>;
+++		regulator-max-microvolt = <3300000>;
+++		vin-supply = <&vdd_5v>;
+++	};
+++
+++	sdio_pwrseq: sdio-pwrseq {
+++		compatible = "mmc-pwrseq-simple";
+++		clocks = <&rk805 1>;
+++		clock-names = "ext_clock";
+++		pinctrl-names = "default";
+++		pinctrl-0 = <&wifi_reg_on_h>;
+++		reset-gpios = <&gpio3 RK_PB0 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+++	};
++ };
++ 
++ &emmc {
++@@ -51,3 +70,43 @@
++ 	vccio4-supply = <&vcc_io_33>;
++ 	vccio6-supply = <&vcc_18>;
++ };
+++
+++&pinctrl {
+++	wifi {
+++		wifi_host_wake_h: wifi-host-wake-h {
+++			rockchip,pins = <3 RK_PA1 RK_FUNC_GPIO &pcfg_pull_down>;
+++		};
+++
+++		wifi_reg_on_h: wifi-reg-on-h {
+++			rockchip,pins = <3 RK_PB0 RK_FUNC_GPIO &pcfg_pull_none>;
+++		};
+++	};
+++};
+++
+++&sdmmc_ext {
+++	#address-cells = <1>;
+++	#size-cells = <0>;
+++	bus-width = <4>;
+++	cap-sd-highspeed;
+++	cap-sdio-irq;
+++	keep-power-in-suspend;
+++	mmc-pwrseq = <&sdio_pwrseq>;
+++	no-mmc;
+++	no-sd;
+++	non-removable;
+++	pinctrl-names = "default";
+++	pinctrl-0 = <&sdmmc0ext_clk &sdmmc0ext_cmd &sdmmc0ext_bus4>;
+++	sd-uhs-sdr104;
+++	vmmc-supply = <&vdd_wifi_3v3>;
+++	vqmmc-supply = <&vcc_18>;
+++	status = "okay";
+++
+++	rtl8822cs: wifi@1 {
+++		reg = <1>;
+++		interrupt-parent = <&gpio3>;
+++		interrupts = <RK_PA1 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+++		interrupt-names = "host-wake";
+++		pinctrl-names = "default";
+++		pinctrl-0 = <&wifi_host_wake_h>;
+++	};
+++};
diff --git a/patches/openwrt/0024-mac80211-rtw88-backport-AP-mode-fixes-from-rtw-next.patch b/patches/openwrt/0024-mac80211-rtw88-backport-AP-mode-fixes-from-rtw-next.patch
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..22a1ef22
--- /dev/null
+++ b/patches/openwrt/0024-mac80211-rtw88-backport-AP-mode-fixes-from-rtw-next.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,386 @@
+From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
+Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 13:45:58 +0200
+Subject: mac80211: rtw88: backport AP mode fixes from rtw-next
+
+In AP mode rtw88 routes every broadcast and multicast data frame
+through the chip's after-DTIM queue. The queue drains only a few frames
+per DTIM, so ordinary multicast chatter fills the shared TX page pool
+and the beacon download fails. rtw-next fixed this for USB and widened
+the ATIM window for all buses.
+
+Backport the four commits: c710c7c2e038 ("wifi: rtw88: usb: route bmc
+frames via the high queue only for DTIM delivery"), 04cc8c59a3d7
+("wifi: rtw88: usb: bound what the driver feeds the after-DTIM queue"),
+185971f1b4eb ("wifi: rtw88: usb: only let the frames a dozing station
+needs use the after-DTIM queue") and cf8b516f6765 ("wifi: rtw88: widen
+the ATIM window while an AP interface is up").
+
+Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
+Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5-1
+
+diff --git a/package/kernel/mac80211/Makefile b/package/kernel/mac80211/Makefile
+index 40aac6fd26bbc3f17b1a2b29ec532e537f405fc0..04af0748d98dd030f5f020f463d3cfdda6b96e8d 100644
+--- a/package/kernel/mac80211/Makefile
++++ b/package/kernel/mac80211/Makefile
+@@ -12,7 +12,7 @@ PKG_NAME:=mac80211
+ 
+ PKG_SOURCE_VERSION:=7.2
+ PKG_VERSION:=$(subst -,_,$(PKG_SOURCE_VERSION))
+-PKG_RELEASE:=13
++PKG_RELEASE:=14
+ PKG_LICENSE:=GPL-2.0-only
+ PKG_LICENSE_FILES:=COPYING
+ 
+diff --git a/package/kernel/mac80211/patches/rtl/004-wifi-rtw88-usb-route-bmc-frames-via-the-high-queue-only-for-DTIM-delivery.patch b/package/kernel/mac80211/patches/rtl/004-wifi-rtw88-usb-route-bmc-frames-via-the-high-queue-only-for-DTIM-delivery.patch
+new file mode 100644
+index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..3a542d88e2bacedf6bc547ad75ee5ba3edaaba3d
+--- /dev/null
++++ b/package/kernel/mac80211/patches/rtl/004-wifi-rtw88-usb-route-bmc-frames-via-the-high-queue-only-for-DTIM-delivery.patch
+@@ -0,0 +1,63 @@
++From c710c7c2e0380d7c0eeb33b686262edcaef7cb07 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
++From: Mehmet Fide <mehmet.fide@screeningeagle.com>
++Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 07:34:26 +0200
++Subject: [PATCH] wifi: rtw88: usb: route bmc frames via the high queue only
++ for DTIM delivery
++
++In AP mode every broadcast and multicast data frame is routed to
++TX_DESC_QSEL_HIGH, the after-DTIM queue, whether or not anybody is
++asleep. The firmware drains that queue at beacon pace, a dozen or so
++frames per second measured on RTL8822BU, while one associated client's
++mDNS/SSDP chatter alone exceeds that. The excess accumulates inside
++the chip until the shared TX page pool is exhausted (measured: 14 of
++1803 pages left). From that point every host-sourced frame queues
++behind the backlog: authentication responses reach the air seconds
++after the client has given up, so no station can associate anymore,
++and the beacon reserved-page download fails the BCN_VALID poll
++("error beacon valid") because it needs pages from the same pool. The
++AP keeps beaconing, so the failure looks like a silent RX stall and
++only a reboot recovers.
++
++mac80211 already decides when after-DTIM delivery is needed: it sets
++IEEE80211_TX_CTL_SEND_AFTER_DTIM on bmc frames only while at least one
++station is actually dozing. Honor that instead of routing
++unconditionally: flagged frames keep going through the high queue with
++the MORE_DATA and HGQMD handling introduced by commit 076f786a0ae1
++("wifi: rtw88: Fix AP mode incorrect DTIM behavior"), everything else
++leaves at line rate through the AC queues. This partially reverts the
++usb.c hunk of that commit, whose unconditional routing is what lets
++the backlog build up.
++
++On a bench AP (USB2, 20 MHz, WPA2, hostapd, a Windows client driven
++through disconnect/reconnect cycles): reconnects fail 0/5 on RTL8822BU
++and 0/3 on RTL8821CU before this change, and pass 10/10 and 5/5 with
++it, with the page pool staying healthy and no beacon errors logged.
++
++Signed-off-by: Mehmet Fide <mehmet.fide@screeningeagle.com>
++Acked-by: Ping-Ke Shih <pkshih@realtek.com>
++Signed-off-by: Ping-Ke Shih <pkshih@realtek.com>
++Link: https://patch.msgid.link/20260814053426.2473247-1-mehmet.fide@gmail.com
++---
++ drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/usb.c | 4 +++-
++ 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)
++
++--- a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/usb.c
+++++ b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/usb.c
++@@ -610,6 +610,7 @@ static int rtw_usb_write_data_h2c(struct
++ static u8 rtw_usb_tx_queue_mapping_to_qsel(struct sk_buff *skb)
++ {
++ 	struct ieee80211_hdr *hdr = (struct ieee80211_hdr *)skb->data;
+++	struct ieee80211_tx_info *info = IEEE80211_SKB_CB(skb);
++ 	__le16 fc = hdr->frame_control;
++ 	u8 qsel;
++ 
++@@ -617,7 +618,8 @@ static u8 rtw_usb_tx_queue_mapping_to_qs
++ 		qsel = TX_DESC_QSEL_MGMT;
++ 	else if (is_broadcast_ether_addr(hdr->addr1) ||
++ 		 is_multicast_ether_addr(hdr->addr1))
++-		qsel = TX_DESC_QSEL_HIGH;
+++		qsel = (info->flags & IEEE80211_TX_CTL_SEND_AFTER_DTIM) ?
+++		       TX_DESC_QSEL_HIGH : skb->priority;
++ 	else if (skb_get_queue_mapping(skb) <= IEEE80211_AC_BK)
++ 		qsel = skb->priority;
++ 	else
+diff --git a/package/kernel/mac80211/patches/rtl/005-wifi-rtw88-usb-bound-what-the-driver-feeds-the-after-DTIM-queue.patch b/package/kernel/mac80211/patches/rtl/005-wifi-rtw88-usb-bound-what-the-driver-feeds-the-after-DTIM-queue.patch
+new file mode 100644
+index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..53006b80df55aadfa628d1d87024a6148eaa32c1
+--- /dev/null
++++ b/package/kernel/mac80211/patches/rtl/005-wifi-rtw88-usb-bound-what-the-driver-feeds-the-after-DTIM-queue.patch
+@@ -0,0 +1,119 @@
++From 04cc8c59a3d79ef7ec2341f1c7aef1d40a0ce7df Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
++From: Mehmet Fide <mehmet.fide@screeningeagle.com>
++Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 10:17:14 +0200
++Subject: [PATCH] wifi: rtw88: usb: bound what the driver feeds the after-DTIM
++ queue
++
++Frames routed to the high queue are transmitted right after DTIM beacons
++only, inside the ATIM window, while they wait in the shared TX page pool.
++The driver puts no limit on how many it hands over, so as long as one
++station dozes, any sustained broadcast or multicast traffic outruns the
++drain and empties the pool: measured on an RTL8822BU AP with a single
++client in power save and ~40 frames/s of mDNS chatter, the free page
++count at 0x240 goes from 1803 to 16 in about 100 seconds and stays there
++for as long as the traffic lasts. From that point every other transmit
++queues behind the backlog, authentication responses arrive too late for
++anyone to join, and the reserved page download fails ("error beacon
++valid"). The AP keeps beaconing and only a reboot recovers.
++
++Feed the high queue through a small budget that refills below the
++measured drain rate (about 3 frames per DTIM, ~15/s at dtim_period 2 on
++the default 2 TU ATIM window); whatever exceeds the budget leaves on its
++access category queue right away. The high queue backlog is now bounded
++by the burst size under any load, so the page pool cannot run dry, at
++the price that a dozing station may miss part of a broadcast storm.
++
++Signed-off-by: Mehmet Fide <mehmet.fide@screeningeagle.com>
++Acked-by: Ping-Ke Shih <pkshih@realtek.com>
++Signed-off-by: Ping-Ke Shih <pkshih@realtek.com>
++Link: https://patch.msgid.link/20260910081716.769081-2-mehmet.fide@gmail.com
++---
++ drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/usb.c | 41 ++++++++++++++++++++++--
++ drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/usb.h |  5 +++
++ 2 files changed, 43 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)
++
++--- a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/usb.c
+++++ b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/usb.c
++@@ -607,7 +607,37 @@ static int rtw_usb_write_data_h2c(struct
++ 	return rtw_usb_write_data(rtwdev, &pkt_info, buf, false);
++ }
++ 
++-static u8 rtw_usb_tx_queue_mapping_to_qsel(struct sk_buff *skb)
+++#define RTW_USB_HIQ_REFILL_INTERVAL	(HZ / 10)	/* one unit of budget per 100 ms */
+++#define RTW_USB_HIQ_BUDGET_MAX		16
+++
+++static bool rtw_usb_hiq_take_budget(struct rtw_usb *rtwusb)
+++{
+++	unsigned long flags, elapsed, add;
+++	bool ok;
+++
+++	spin_lock_irqsave(&rtwusb->hiq_lock, flags);
+++
+++	elapsed = jiffies - rtwusb->hiq_refill;
+++	add = elapsed / RTW_USB_HIQ_REFILL_INTERVAL;
+++	if (add) {
+++		rtwusb->hiq_budget = min_t(unsigned long, rtwusb->hiq_budget + add,
+++					   RTW_USB_HIQ_BUDGET_MAX);
+++
+++		/* keep the unfinished part of the interval for the next unit */
+++		rtwusb->hiq_refill = jiffies - elapsed % RTW_USB_HIQ_REFILL_INTERVAL;
+++	}
+++
+++	ok = rtwusb->hiq_budget > 0;
+++	if (ok)
+++		rtwusb->hiq_budget--;
+++
+++	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&rtwusb->hiq_lock, flags);
+++
+++	return ok;
+++}
+++
+++static u8 rtw_usb_tx_queue_mapping_to_qsel(struct rtw_usb *rtwusb,
+++					   struct sk_buff *skb)
++ {
++ 	struct ieee80211_hdr *hdr = (struct ieee80211_hdr *)skb->data;
++ 	struct ieee80211_tx_info *info = IEEE80211_SKB_CB(skb);
++@@ -618,7 +648,8 @@ static u8 rtw_usb_tx_queue_mapping_to_qs
++ 		qsel = TX_DESC_QSEL_MGMT;
++ 	else if (is_broadcast_ether_addr(hdr->addr1) ||
++ 		 is_multicast_ether_addr(hdr->addr1))
++-		qsel = (info->flags & IEEE80211_TX_CTL_SEND_AFTER_DTIM) ?
+++		qsel = (info->flags & IEEE80211_TX_CTL_SEND_AFTER_DTIM) &&
+++		       rtw_usb_hiq_take_budget(rtwusb) ?
++ 		       TX_DESC_QSEL_HIGH : skb->priority;
++ 	else if (skb_get_queue_mapping(skb) <= IEEE80211_AC_BK)
++ 		qsel = skb->priority;
++@@ -638,7 +669,7 @@ static int rtw_usb_tx_write(struct rtw_d
++ 	u8 *pkt_desc;
++ 	int ep;
++ 
++-	pkt_info->qsel = rtw_usb_tx_queue_mapping_to_qsel(skb);
+++	pkt_info->qsel = rtw_usb_tx_queue_mapping_to_qsel(rtwusb, skb);
++ 	pkt_desc = skb_push(skb, chip->tx_pkt_desc_sz);
++ 	memset(pkt_desc, 0, chip->tx_pkt_desc_sz);
++ 	ep = qsel_to_ep(rtwusb, pkt_info->qsel);
++@@ -1079,6 +1110,10 @@ static int rtw_usb_init_tx(struct rtw_de
++ 	struct rtw_usb *rtwusb = rtw_get_usb_priv(rtwdev);
++ 	int i;
++ 
+++	spin_lock_init(&rtwusb->hiq_lock);
+++	rtwusb->hiq_budget = RTW_USB_HIQ_BUDGET_MAX;
+++	rtwusb->hiq_refill = jiffies;
+++
++ 	rtwusb->txwq = create_singlethread_workqueue("rtw88_usb: tx wq");
++ 	if (!rtwusb->txwq) {
++ 		rtw_err(rtwdev, "failed to create TX work queue\n");
++--- a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/usb.h
+++++ b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/usb.h
++@@ -75,6 +75,11 @@ struct rtw_usb {
++ 	u8 out_ep[RTW_USB_EP_MAX];
++ 	int qsel_to_ep[TX_DESC_QSEL_MAX];
++ 
+++	/* protects hiq_budget and hiq_refill */
+++	spinlock_t hiq_lock;
+++	u32 hiq_budget;
+++	unsigned long hiq_refill;
+++
++ 	struct workqueue_struct *txwq, *rxwq;
++ 
++ 	struct sk_buff_head tx_queue[RTW_USB_EP_MAX];
+diff --git a/package/kernel/mac80211/patches/rtl/006-wifi-rtw88-usb-only-let-the-frames-a-dozing-station-needs-use-the-after-DTIM-queue.patch b/package/kernel/mac80211/patches/rtl/006-wifi-rtw88-usb-only-let-the-frames-a-dozing-station-needs-use-the-after-DTIM-queue.patch
+new file mode 100644
+index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..2c3ca894a9007ee7a032a3f414757b7be039cca2
+--- /dev/null
++++ b/package/kernel/mac80211/patches/rtl/006-wifi-rtw88-usb-only-let-the-frames-a-dozing-station-needs-use-the-after-DTIM-queue.patch
+@@ -0,0 +1,88 @@
++From 185971f1b4eb4ea9059d76cf1a18f992eef998c1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
++From: Mehmet Fide <mehmet.fide@screeningeagle.com>
++Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 10:17:15 +0200
++Subject: [PATCH] wifi: rtw88: usb: only let the frames a dozing station needs
++ use the after-DTIM queue
++
++With the budget in place the high queue can no longer take the chip
++down, but ordinary chatter still competes with the frames a sleeping
++station actually needs. mac80211 marks every broadcast and multicast
++frame with IEEE80211_TX_CTL_SEND_AFTER_DTIM while a station dozes,
++mDNS and SSDP included, so under normal traffic the budget is spent on
++frames nobody waits for.
++
++Do what the vendor driver does with its default "allow special" high
++queue filter: admit only ARP, EAPOL and DHCP to the after-DTIM path,
++the frames a station coming out of power save has to see; everything
++else goes out on its access category queue at line rate. The frames
++are classified the way rtw89_core_tx_btc_spec_pkt_notify() does it,
++from skb->protocol and the network and transport header offsets that
++mac80211 keeps from the netdev path; EAPOL is the control port flag.
++Frames the AP relays between its own stations arrive as ETH_P_802_3
++and are not inspected, they leave on the access category queue.
++
++With the filter in place the page pool stays at 1803 through the same
++180 second storm, a DHCP flood still takes the after-DTIM path (and is
++then held by the budget), and join/ping cycling without power save is
++unchanged (10/10).
++
++Signed-off-by: Mehmet Fide <mehmet.fide@screeningeagle.com>
++Acked-by: Ping-Ke Shih <pkshih@realtek.com>
++Signed-off-by: Ping-Ke Shih <pkshih@realtek.com>
++Link: https://patch.msgid.link/20260910081716.769081-3-mehmet.fide@gmail.com
++---
++ drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/usb.c | 27 ++++++++++++++++++++++--
++ 1 file changed, 25 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)
++
++--- a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/usb.c
+++++ b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/usb.c
++@@ -2,9 +2,11 @@
++ /* Copyright(c) 2018-2019  Realtek Corporation
++  */
++ 
+++#include <linux/ip.h>
++ #include <linux/module.h>
++-#include <linux/usb.h>
++ #include <linux/mutex.h>
+++#include <linux/udp.h>
+++#include <linux/usb.h>
++ #include "main.h"
++ #include "debug.h"
++ #include "mac.h"
++@@ -607,6 +609,27 @@ static int rtw_usb_write_data_h2c(struct
++ 	return rtw_usb_write_data(rtwdev, &pkt_info, buf, false);
++ }
++ 
+++static bool rtw_usb_bmc_needs_dtim(struct sk_buff *skb)
+++{
+++	struct ieee80211_tx_info *info = IEEE80211_SKB_CB(skb);
+++	struct udphdr *udphdr;
+++
+++	if (info->control.flags & IEEE80211_TX_CTRL_PORT_CTRL_PROTO)
+++		return true;
+++
+++	if (skb->protocol == htons(ETH_P_ARP))
+++		return true;
+++
+++	if (skb->protocol == htons(ETH_P_IP) &&
+++	    ip_hdr(skb)->protocol == IPPROTO_UDP) {
+++		udphdr = udp_hdr(skb);
+++
+++		return udphdr->dest == htons(67) || udphdr->dest == htons(68);
+++	}
+++
+++	return false;
+++}
+++
++ #define RTW_USB_HIQ_REFILL_INTERVAL	(HZ / 10)	/* one unit of budget per 100 ms */
++ #define RTW_USB_HIQ_BUDGET_MAX		16
++ 
++@@ -649,7 +672,7 @@ static u8 rtw_usb_tx_queue_mapping_to_qs
++ 	else if (is_broadcast_ether_addr(hdr->addr1) ||
++ 		 is_multicast_ether_addr(hdr->addr1))
++ 		qsel = (info->flags & IEEE80211_TX_CTL_SEND_AFTER_DTIM) &&
++-		       rtw_usb_hiq_take_budget(rtwusb) ?
+++		       rtw_usb_bmc_needs_dtim(skb) && rtw_usb_hiq_take_budget(rtwusb) ?
++ 		       TX_DESC_QSEL_HIGH : skb->priority;
++ 	else if (skb_get_queue_mapping(skb) <= IEEE80211_AC_BK)
++ 		qsel = skb->priority;
+diff --git a/package/kernel/mac80211/patches/rtl/007-wifi-rtw88-widen-the-ATIM-window-while-an-AP-interface-is-up.patch b/package/kernel/mac80211/patches/rtl/007-wifi-rtw88-widen-the-ATIM-window-while-an-AP-interface-is-up.patch
+new file mode 100644
+index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..e1da9137075eb74ec388e352d9dc369906b08acd
+--- /dev/null
++++ b/package/kernel/mac80211/patches/rtl/007-wifi-rtw88-widen-the-ATIM-window-while-an-AP-interface-is-up.patch
+@@ -0,0 +1,59 @@
++From cf8b516f67652f550e040951b88a2040b375afcf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
++From: Mehmet Fide <mehmet.fide@screeningeagle.com>
++Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 10:17:16 +0200
++Subject: [PATCH] wifi: rtw88: widen the ATIM window while an AP interface is
++ up
++
++The frames held for dozing stations go out right after the DTIM beacon
++and only for the duration of the ATIM window. The chips come up with a
++2 TU window (the two that program it set 0x2 at init), which drains
++about three frames per DTIM; the vendor driver sets 0xa or 0xc in AP
++mode instead. With the 2 TU window the measured page pool exhaustion
++takes ~100 s of ordinary multicast chatter; at 0x4 the same storm
++already drains, so 0xa leaves comfortable headroom.
++
++Program 0xa when an AP interface starts and put the default back when
++it stops, since most chips never initialize the register themselves.
++The bound and the filter of the previous patches still apply: the
++window adds drain capacity, the budget protects the pool when that is
++not enough, the filter keeps chatter off the beacon-paced path.
++
++Suggested-by: Ping-Ke Shih <pkshih@realtek.com>
++Signed-off-by: Mehmet Fide <mehmet.fide@screeningeagle.com>
++Acked-by: Ping-Ke Shih <pkshih@realtek.com>
++Signed-off-by: Ping-Ke Shih <pkshih@realtek.com>
++Link: https://patch.msgid.link/20260910081716.769081-4-mehmet.fide@gmail.com
++---
++ drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/mac80211.c | 2 ++
++ drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/reg.h      | 2 ++
++ 2 files changed, 4 insertions(+)
++
++--- a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/mac80211.c
+++++ b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/mac80211.c
++@@ -468,6 +468,7 @@ static int rtw_ops_start_ap(struct ieee8
++ 
++ 	mutex_lock(&rtwdev->mutex);
++ 	rtw_write32_set(rtwdev, REG_TCR, BIT_TCR_UPDATE_HGQMD);
+++	rtw_write16(rtwdev, REG_ATIMWND, ATIMWND_AP);
++ 	rtwdev->ap_active = true;
++ 	rtw_store_op_chan(rtwdev, true);
++ 	chip->ops->phy_calibration(rtwdev);
++@@ -484,6 +485,7 @@ static void rtw_ops_stop_ap(struct ieee8
++ 
++ 	mutex_lock(&rtwdev->mutex);
++ 	rtw_write32_clr(rtwdev, REG_TCR, BIT_TCR_UPDATE_HGQMD);
+++	rtw_write16(rtwdev, REG_ATIMWND, ATIMWND_DEFAULT);
++ 	rtwdev->ap_active = false;
++ 	if (!rtw_core_check_sta_active(rtwdev))
++ 		rtw_clear_op_chan(rtwdev);
++--- a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/reg.h
+++++ b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/reg.h
++@@ -483,6 +483,8 @@
++ #define REG_DRVERLYINT		0x0558
++ #define REG_BCNDMATIM		0x0559
++ #define REG_ATIMWND		0x055A
+++#define ATIMWND_DEFAULT		0x2
+++#define ATIMWND_AP		0xa
++ #define REG_USTIME_TSF		0x055C
++ #define REG_BCN_MAX_ERR		0x055D
++ #define REG_RXTSF_OFFSET_CCK	0x055E
diff --git a/patches/openwrt/0025-mac80211-rtw88-fix-AP-mode-stall-on-SDIO-and-PCIe.patch b/patches/openwrt/0025-mac80211-rtw88-fix-AP-mode-stall-on-SDIO-and-PCIe.patch
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..f62e9030
--- /dev/null
+++ b/patches/openwrt/0025-mac80211-rtw88-fix-AP-mode-stall-on-SDIO-and-PCIe.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,132 @@
+From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
+Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 13:45:59 +0200
+Subject: mac80211: rtw88: fix AP mode stall on SDIO and PCIe
+
+The shared queue mapping of the SDIO and PCIe paths still sends every
+broadcast and multicast frame to the after-DTIM queue. On the RTL8822CS
+of the NanoPi R2S Plus the TX page pool empties within seconds of a
+client associating, the beacon download fails every few seconds and
+the link stalls with round trip times of hundreds of milliseconds and
+hanging TCP connections.
+
+Map only frames that carry IEEE80211_TX_CTL_SEND_AFTER_DTIM to the
+after-DTIM queue, as the USB path does since the backported fix. With
+the change the page pool stays full, round trip times are 2 to 3 ms and
+TCP reaches about 60 Mbit/s in each direction in AP mode, also over a
+10 minute soak with a dozing client. The patch goes to linux-wireless.
+
+Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
+Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5-1
+
+diff --git a/package/kernel/mac80211/Makefile b/package/kernel/mac80211/Makefile
+index 04af0748d98dd030f5f020f463d3cfdda6b96e8d..19e7c577e48ab6dfe8c18b2e5e97b26ea7210375 100644
+--- a/package/kernel/mac80211/Makefile
++++ b/package/kernel/mac80211/Makefile
+@@ -12,7 +12,7 @@ PKG_NAME:=mac80211
+ 
+ PKG_SOURCE_VERSION:=7.2
+ PKG_VERSION:=$(subst -,_,$(PKG_SOURCE_VERSION))
+-PKG_RELEASE:=14
++PKG_RELEASE:=15
+ PKG_LICENSE:=GPL-2.0-only
+ PKG_LICENSE_FILES:=COPYING
+ 
+diff --git a/package/kernel/mac80211/patches/rtl/008-wifi-rtw88-honor-SEND_AFTER_DTIM-for-bmc-frames-on-SDIO-and-PCIe.patch b/package/kernel/mac80211/patches/rtl/008-wifi-rtw88-honor-SEND_AFTER_DTIM-for-bmc-frames-on-SDIO-and-PCIe.patch
+new file mode 100644
+index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..723d3da9c9a8777ea0da5b9815709386fe7c0e19
+--- /dev/null
++++ b/package/kernel/mac80211/patches/rtl/008-wifi-rtw88-honor-SEND_AFTER_DTIM-for-bmc-frames-on-SDIO-and-PCIe.patch
+@@ -0,0 +1,93 @@
++From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
++Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 20:43:03 +0200
++Subject: [PATCH] wifi: rtw88: honor SEND_AFTER_DTIM for bmc frames on SDIO and
++ PCIe
++
++rtw_tx_queue_mapping() puts every broadcast and multicast data frame
++into RTW_TX_QUEUE_HI0. The SDIO and PCIe paths use this function. The
++high queue is the after DTIM queue of the chip. In AP mode the hardware
++holds these frames and sends them only right after a DTIM beacon,
++inside the ATIM window. The default window of 2 TU drains about three
++frames per DTIM.
++
++While the frames wait they occupy pages of the shared TX page pool. On
++RTL8822CS the high queue owns 64 pages and then takes from the 1681
++public pages. Any sustained broadcast or multicast rate above the drain
++rate fills the pool. The beacon and reserved page download and the H2C
++queue draw from the same pool. Once the pool is empty the SDIO free
++page check rejects them with EBUSY. The driver then logs "failed to
++write data to rsvd page", "failed to download drv rsvd page" and
++"failed to download beacon" on every TIM update. The data queues lose
++their share of the public pages as well and the link stalls. The SDIO
++TX worker retries a starved queue a thousand times with three register
++reads per attempt before it moves on. The stall therefore also shows up
++as a burst of SDIO commands and long ping times.
++
++mac80211 already decides which frames need after DTIM delivery. It sets
++IEEE80211_TX_CTL_SEND_AFTER_DTIM on a broadcast or multicast frame only
++while at least one station is dozing. Map only those frames to the high
++queue. Everything else leaves on its access category queue at line
++rate. This is the SDIO and PCIe counterpart of
++commit c710c7c2e038 ("wifi: rtw88: usb: route bmc frames via the high
++queue only for DTIM delivery"). That commit made the same change in the
++USB queue mapping. The vendor driver behaves the same way. It only
++selects the high queue for frames it has buffered for a sleeping
++station.
++
++The change has one known trade-off. Group frames that already wait in
++the high queue when the last dozing station wakes up still leave after
++the next DTIM, while later group frames leave at once on their access
++category queue. Once per wake transition group frames can therefore
++arrive out of order, bounded by what the high queue holds for one DTIM
++interval. On a protected network the client drops the late frames as
++replays, because the group PN is assigned in software in submission
++order. The USB path has accepted the same trade-off since
++commit c710c7c2e038 ("wifi: rtw88: usb: route bmc frames via the high
++queue only for DTIM delivery"). Before this change the frames stayed
++in order, but the link stalled.
++
++Observed on an RTL8822CS in a NanoPi R2S Plus running as an AP with one
++client. Before this change the reserved page download failed every few
++seconds, ping times reached 770 ms and TCP connections timed out.
++With this change the same setup shows no page starvation and no
++reserved page failure. Ping times are 2 to 3 ms with the client awake
++and TCP throughput is about 60 Mbit/s in each direction, also during a
++10 minute soak with the client in power save. A dozing client together
++with about 250 group frames per second still fills the pool. That case
++needs the budget and the filter that the USB path already has.
++STA mode was not affected because a station never addresses data frames
++to a group address. The PCIe path shares this mapping and was not tested
++by the author, so Realtek may want to confirm the change on a PCIe part.
++
++The analysis of the stall and the first draft of this patch and its
++changelog were produced in Claude Code sessions with the Claude model
++claude-fable-5-1. The same sessions ran the tests on an RTL8822CS in a
++NanoPi R2S Plus. The author reviewed the change and the results.
++
++Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
++---
++ drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/tx.c | 6 ++++--
++ 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)
++
++--- a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/tx.c
+++++ b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/tx.c
++@@ -725,6 +725,7 @@ EXPORT_SYMBOL(rtw_tx_ac_to_hwq);
++ enum rtw_tx_queue_type rtw_tx_queue_mapping(struct sk_buff *skb)
++ {
++ 	struct ieee80211_hdr *hdr = (struct ieee80211_hdr *)skb->data;
+++	struct ieee80211_tx_info *info = IEEE80211_SKB_CB(skb);
++ 	__le16 fc = hdr->frame_control;
++ 	u8 q_mapping = skb_get_queue_mapping(skb);
++ 	enum rtw_tx_queue_type queue;
++@@ -733,8 +734,9 @@ enum rtw_tx_queue_type rtw_tx_queue_mapp
++ 		queue = RTW_TX_QUEUE_BCN;
++ 	else if (unlikely(ieee80211_is_mgmt(fc) || ieee80211_is_ctl(fc)))
++ 		queue = RTW_TX_QUEUE_MGMT;
++-	else if (is_broadcast_ether_addr(hdr->addr1) ||
++-		 is_multicast_ether_addr(hdr->addr1))
