+--- /dev/null
++++ b/package/kernel/mac80211/patches/rtl/009-wifi-rtw88-add-802.11s-mesh-point-support.patch
+@@ -0,0 +1,364 @@
++From: Christian Schmidbauer <opensource@grische.xyz>
++Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 16:00:28 +0200
++Subject: [PATCH] wifi: rtw88: add 802.11s mesh point support
++
++Commit 2ccb8e6ce8a4 ("wifi: rtw88: remove unsupported interface type
++of mesh point") removed the mesh point interface type from rtw88. The
++mesh code had never been made to work. Parts of the mesh setup are still
++in place. A mesh interface gets the AP net type, the beacon function and
++a beacon in the reserved page, just like an AP interface. Several pieces
++are missing.
++
++mac80211 does not call start_ap and stop_ap for a mesh interface. It
++enables the beacon when it joins a mesh and disables it when it leaves.
++The driver therefore never sets ap_active for mesh, never widens the
++ATIM window and never runs the calibration. The reserved page code also
++gathers the beacon of a mesh interface before it joins a mesh. mac80211
++has no beacon for it at that point, so the reserved page download fails.
++mac80211 also disables the beacon for off-channel work, such as a
++software scan or a remain-on-channel, and enables it again afterwards.
++These calls alone therefore do not tell when a mesh starts and ends.
++
++Only port 0 sends beacons. The driver moves an AP interface to port 0,
++but not a mesh interface. A mesh interface that is the only interface
++gets port 0 anyway.
++
++A mesh peer puts its own address into the BSSID field of its beacons.
++The driver sets BIT_CBSSID_BCN after every scan. The hardware then drops
++the beacons of the peers. mac80211 needs them to discover peers and to
++learn their capabilities. mac80211 sets FIF_OTHER_BSS while a mesh
++interface is joined. The Realtek vendor driver clears the BSSID check
++while a mesh interface is up as well.
++
++The RX path updates the RSSI of a station only for frames that carry
++the BSSID of the interface. A mesh interface has no BSSID, so the RSSI
++of every peer stays at zero. The firmware rate adaptation and DIG work
++with that value. The CFO tracking matches frames by BSSID as well, so it
++never adjusts the crystal for mesh peers. Without the BSSID check, on
++the other hand, the beacons of all networks on the channel would count
++as beacons of the interface.
++
++The security CAM finds group keys only by their key index. Each mesh
++peer protects its group frames with its own group key. mac80211 already
++handles these peer keys in software, because rtw88 does not support per
++station group keys. The group key of the local mesh interface would
++still sit in the CAM, and the hardware would apply it to the group
++frames of the peers. The group key of a station interface on the same
++radio would be applied to them in the same way.
++
++Advertise NL80211_IFTYPE_MESH_POINT on the 8822C, which is the only
++chip this was tested on. Leave it out of the interface combination, so
++that cfg80211 allows a mesh interface only as the single interface on
++the radio, as it does for IBSS. Use software crypto for all keys of a
++mesh interface.
++
++Do the AP setup when mac80211 enables the beacon of a mesh interface.
++Keep it while the beacon is paused for off-channel work, as for an AP
++interface, and only download the beacon again when the pause ends. Undo
++the AP setup when the mesh interface becomes idle, which it does when
++mac80211 releases the channel after leaving the mesh, also during a
++scan. Until then ap_active stays set without a beacon, which is the same
++short window an AP interface has before stop_ap. A restart of the
++hardware resets the AP setup, so clear ap_active when mac80211 starts
++the hardware. The setup is then repeated when the beacon is enabled
++again, also when a pause delays that until after the restart. Treat a
++mesh interface like an AP interface for the port switch and the reserved
++page.
++
++Keep the BSSID check off while FIF_OTHER_BSS is set, also at the end of
++a hardware or software scan. When mac80211 drops FIF_OTHER_BSS after
++leaving the mesh, turn the beacon BSSID check back on, unless
++FIF_BCN_PRBRESP_PROMISC is still set. For a mesh interface, use only
++the frames of known peers for the RX statistics, the RSSI and the CFO
++tracking.
++
++This makes 802.11s mesh point available on the 8822C. A mesh interface
++next to an AP interface would need a second beacon, but the driver
++keeps a single beacon in the reserved page and beacons only on port 0.
++
++This was tested on a NanoPi R2S Plus with an RTL8822CS on SDIO at
++50 MHz, with the driver from backports 7.2 and an ath10k peer on
++channel 36 at 80 MHz. An open mesh reached ESTAB on both sides, also
++after scans and after each of eight leave and join cycles. Ping took
++2 to 10 ms, and iperf3 reached 55 to 61 Mbit/s towards the board and
++27 Mbit/s from it. A mesh with SAE reached 46 Mbit/s towards the
++board and 28 Mbit/s from it with all keys in software. cfg80211 in
++backports 7.2 predates commit 8ff047b9c7b2 ("wifi: cfg80211: clarify
++and tighten key checks"), so that run needed WIPHY_FLAG_IBSS_RSN for
++the group keys of the peer. A station or AP interface next to the
++mesh interface was refused in either order. The mesh recovered from a
++firmware restart. After a restart during a remain-on-channel the AP
++setup was repeated when the pause ended, which it was not without the
++ap_active change. Since mac80211 currently loses the end of a
++software remain-on-channel over a restart, a second remain-on-channel
++request was used to bring it back. AP and station mode performed as
++without the patch.
++
++An earlier form of this patch also ran floods of 50 and 200 broadcast
++frames per second, a 10 minute soak and batman-adv with BATMAN_V
++without TX page shortage, beacon download failure or lost peering.
++
++Signed-off-by: Christian Schmidbauer <opensource@grische.xyz>
